{"kind":"assert","text":"Layering and simplification pass over the two merge-ready heads: task/session-privacy at 62c20a5445c461080dc0da8f212fde409992caa7 and task/worktree-visibility at dfa02060c3e3b9f2c0c052150a28ba3491ca47d4. The one behavioural defect is in dissent ff3ab05b31907375445c0049d0beb422109af6d1; what follows is structure, and none of it is a merge blocker on its own.\n\nLAYERING.\n\nL1. LocalWorktrees is in the wrong package. Package app is documented as joining the semantic-free kernel to the workroom profile — the record layer. LocalWorktrees shells out to git, canonicalises filesystem paths and caches the result, and I checked its coupling rather than guessed: it touches w.Repo, a string, and its own three cache fields, and nothing else. Not Store, not Config, not the kernel, not the workroom profile. It has one caller, service/ui.go:74. The tell is that WorktreeView's own doc comment has to say \"never part of the durable workroom projection\" — a comment defending against a confusion a package boundary would prevent for free. Move it to its own small package over a repo path and that claim becomes a fact about the import graph.\n\nL2. Workspace now holds two caches with unrelated invalidation models: snapshotCache keyed on the git ref head, worktreesCached keyed on wall-clock age. Anyone reading the struct has to carry both. L1 takes this with it.\n\nL3. In session-privacy, the never-publish-a-credential invariant is enforced by discipline rather than by types. Five call sites must each remember to pass the handle and not the id — announceFor's renewal branch, announceFor's append, Depart, expire, Snapshot — and a sixth added later gets it wrong by default. A distinct type, say Handle string, on Change.ID makes append(\"presence\", id, value) fail to compile. One type declaration converts five remembered rules into a compiler-enforced one, which is worth more than the test that currently guards it.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION.\n\nS1. In worktrees.ts, expectedHead is fully derived from expectedHeads — it is the single element when there is exactly one. It costs a field on the exported interface, a branch in the producer, a prop on WorktreePill, and a line in the tooltip builder, and both are only ever used to compose one string. Drop it.\n\nS2. allExpected is rebuilt inside the per-worktree loop but does not depend on the worktree. Hoist it.\n\nS3. The evidence chain assigns \"durable\" twice in sequence, which reads as though the second case might differ. One boolean over the two durable conditions says it plainly.\n\nS4. LocalWorktrees does six jobs in one function: cache, exec, parse, bound, canonicalise, inspect, sort. The parse is where all the subtlety lives — the NUL framing, spaces in paths, bare, locked, prunable, detached — and it is currently only reachable by building real repositories on disk, which is how I had to probe it. Extracting a pure parse over the porcelain bytes makes those cases table-driven, and makes a future unknown key a test rather than a discovery.\n\nS5. Two bounded surfaces on the same branch bound differently. The graph returns its newest eighty with truncated true and the drawer says so; the worktree list returns an error past 128, which the UI renders as \"Local checkout state unavailable\" — the same message a git failure produces, so a large repository looks broken rather than large. The branch already built the better pattern; use it in both places.\n\nMERGE ORDER. All three of session-privacy, worktree-visibility and docs-era merge-tree clean against main and against each other, so ordering is free. One thing not to lose: main's docs/reference.md still states the MCP adapter is modern-only and that clients built against 2025-11-25 or earlier cannot attach. That has been false since the dual-era adapter merged at c89fb8f. session-privacy carries the stale paragraph unchanged rather than making it worse, and docs-era is the branch that corrects it.\n\nSeparately, and pre-existing rather than introduced: DELETE /v0/presence/\u003canything\u003e on a session that never existed advances the nexus cursor and broadcasts a departure to every observer. On main the change echoed the caller's own guess; on this branch it carries an empty id, so repeated phantom departures now collide on \"\" for any client keying by id. Three calls took the cursor from 0 to 3 in both. session-privacy owns this code path today, and the fix is to return the current cursor without appending when the session is not present."}